WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.930
This is a Carfax for New York apartments: a building's public record,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.030
on one card, built in Rust.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.200
Before you sign a lease,

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.100
the records that show whether a building is cared for are already public.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.700
They're just spread across four city agencies.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:21.440
First, it asks what matters to you, and those sections move to the top.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.250
I type an address and pick it from the suggestions.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:30.150
The app covers two hundred fifty Brooklyn buildings, and it's served live.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.070
Thirty-nine out of one hundred, across condition,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.530
legal protection, neighborhood, and accessibility.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.910
Every number links back to the city dataset it came from.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.950
Condition scores zero: one hundred eight hazardous violations are open.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.910
Each one is listed in the city's own words, with how long it's been open.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.550
How fast repairs happen is the one measure of behavior, not just state.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.530
There are three hundred open violations here.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:04.530
And you can take the record with you: print it, or download the file.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.270
The downloaded file is the verifiable one.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:13.530
Here's that exact file, checked offline with HouseCheck's own verification code.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.910
The hash chain recomputes, the signature is valid,

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.790
and the key matches the one the site publishes.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.070
Signed and intact.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.790
Change one character in one violation, and it's caught, down to the row.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.850
But signing alone wasn't enough.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.390
A forger can rewrite a row, rebuild the whole chain,

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.110
and sign it with their own key, and it still checks out as intact.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.090
I found that hole while verifying it in production.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.820
That's why the signing key is published apart from the documents it signs.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.340
Compare against it, and the forgery is exposed.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:52.720
One hundred seventy-six tests, lint-clean, and running live.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:57.480
Two hundred fifty buildings, about a tenth of a percent of the city.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.400
It's a signal, not a legal ruling.
